{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III was still a child when his father was assassinated in 1152 and he succeeded him as Count of Tripoli. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III was a member of the House of Toulouse and was related to several other prominent nobles in the Crusader states. He was a first cousin of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and was also related to the Princes of Antioch. *Reign* Raymond III's reign as Count of Tripoli was marked by several conflicts with neighboring Muslim states. He was involved in several battles against Nur ad-Din and Saladin who were trying to unite the Muslim states of Syria and Egypt against the Crusaders. In 1174 Raymond III supported the coronation of King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem who was a minor at the time and suffered from leprosy.\n\n*Regency in Jerusalem* Raymond III played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a member of the regency council for King Baldwin IV and was also regent for the king on several occasions. He was a leader of the faction that opposed the influence of the Latin Patriarch of Jerusalem and the Knights Templar. Raymond III's regency was marked by several conflicts with the king's mother Agnes of Courtenay and her allies. He was eventually replaced as regent by Guy of Lusignan who was the husband of the king's sister Sibylla. Raymond III's failure to prevent the rise of Guy of Lusignan ultimately contributed to the downfall of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He died in 1187 without an heir and the County of Tripoli was inherited by his godson Raymond IV who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch.", "logprobs": [{"to